# tcpdrill, and what waiting inside accept() puts on the wire.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TREATING THE FILE AS A REGRESSION TEST FOR
# bsd_accept_once().  IT IS NOT ONE, AND IT WAS MEASURED NOT TO BE.
#
# The behaviour these cases were written for is the one select.c describes
# above bsd_wait_sliced(): nx_tcp_server_socket_accept() with a finite wait
# installs _nx_tcp_connect_cleanup() as its timeout handler, that handler
# winds the listening socket from SYN_RECEIVED back to LISTEN, and the next
# call re-enters the LISTEN block, takes a NEW initial sequence number and
# sends a SECOND SYN+ACK for a handshake that is already half done.
#
# That cannot happen in this tree.  A SYN to a listening port never reaches
# the listening socket at all: nx_tcp_packet_process.c hands it to
# _nx_tcp_syncache_syn_received(), which records a half-open entry, answers it
# itself and commits no socket until the third segment arrives.  The LISTEN
# block in nx_tcp_server_socket_accept() only sends a SYN+ACK when the socket
# is BOUND, and a socket the syncache has bound is past LISTEN by the time the
# mutex is released.  So the block winds the state and bumps a sequence number
# nothing ever puts in a header.
#
# MEASURED, not deduced: running these cases against a build with
# bsd_accept_once() reverted to the finite-wait form produces the same frames,
# with the same flags and the same sequence number, in the same order.  The
# only difference is how long the waits take, because the fix replaced a
# suspend with nx_tcp_socket_state_wait()'s one-tick polling loop -- 1735 ms
# of real time for 1600 ms of asked-for accept timeout before, 3177 ms after.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT, and why the file is still here.  Nothing else in the tree
# covers a BLOCKING accept() with a timeout at all, and these are the two
# properties that would break first if the syncache stopped intercepting the
# SYN: one initial sequence number per handshake however long the application
# waits, and a handshake that still completes across repeated waits.  If a
# later change puts the passive open back on the listening socket, this file
# is where it shows up.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# Waiting on a listener nobody has connected to is silent.  The stack has a
# parked socket and a listen request; neither is a reason to transmit.
case aw01_waiting_on_an_idle_listener_sends_nothing
localport 4741
listen
accept = none wait=400 tries=3
notx 300

# One handshake, several accept waits across it, and one initial sequence
# number for the whole of it.
#
# The first pair of waits happens before the SYN, so the re-entry runs with
# the listening socket in LISTEN, which is the branch that would have sent the
# second SYN+ACK.  The SYN+ACK after it latches the sequence number.  The four
# waits after that are longer than the SYN+ACK retransmission timeout, so the
# stack does resend it -- `txsame` is there because that is correct and a bare
# `notx` would report the stack for doing it.  What `txsame` will not accept is
# a SYN+ACK carrying a different sequence number, which is what re-issuing
# rather than retransmitting looks like from here.
case aw02_one_sequence_number_per_handshake_across_accept_waits
localport 4742
listen
accept = none wait=400 tries=2
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA ack=1 mss=1460
accept = none wait=400 tries=4
txsame SA
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept
